{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30862 - PROTEIN:5854",
  "verbalization": "Robinin is implicated in the regulation of LNCaP cells through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Robinin acts as a target for multiple compounds, with the most direct link being to LNCaP through the compound (1S,3R,6S,8R,11S,12S,15R,16R)-15-[(2R)-4-[(2R)-3,3-dimethyloxiran-2-yl]butan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethylpentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecan-6-ol, which is shared as a target between Robinin and LNCaP. This compound is also a target of MIP, another entity linked to Robinin, and is further connected to LNCaP. The presence of multiple pathways converging on LNCaP through this compound suggests a potential mechanism involving the modulation of cellular processes in prostate cancer cells. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Robinin and LNCaP, though the exact molecular mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
